  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 5,
    "lesson_title": "Unequal-Exponent Exponential Convolution",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute the unequal-exponent convolution of two one-sided exponentials by evaluating the causal overlap integral and simplifying it into the scaled difference of exponentials.",
    "section_count": 6,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Unequal-Exponent Exponential Convolution",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Unequal-Exponent Exponential Convolution",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the unequal-exponent case when both signals are one-sided exponentials, but their decay rates are different. The standard form is",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{-at}u(t),\\qquad h(t)=e^{-bt}u(t),\\qquad a>0,\\ b>0,\\ a\\ne b.",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Both unit steps make the signals causal. Inside the convolution integral, $x(\\tau)$ can contribute only when $\\tau \\ge 0$, and $h(t - \\tau)$ can contribute only when $t - \\tau \\ge 0$. For $t \\ge 0$, those two conditions give the overlap interval $0 \\le \\tau \\le t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "(x*h)(t)=\\int_0^t e^{-a\\tau}e^{-b(t-\\tau)}\\,d\\tau,\\qquad t\\ge 0.",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The new feature is the unequal exponent. In the equal-exponent case, the $\\tau$ terms cancel and the integral produces a ramp-weighted exponential. Here the product keeps a $\\tau$-dependent exponential, so the evaluated result becomes a difference of two decays.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "(x*h)(t)=\\frac{e^{-at}-e^{-bt}}{b-a}\\,u(t),\\qquad a\\ne b.",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When you use this pattern, keep the order consistent: the numerator $e^{-at}$-$e^{-bt}$ goes with the denominator b-a. The final $u(t)$ records the no-overlap region, so the output is zero for $t < 0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Unequal-Exponent Exponential Convolution",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Setting Causal Overlap Bounds for Unequal Exponentials",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(t)=e$^{-2t}$u(t)$ and $h(t)=e$^{-5t}$u(t)$, set the causal overlap bounds for (x*h)(t).",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that both signals are one-sided: each formula contains a unit step. In a convolution, rewrite one signal using $\\tau$ and rewrite the shifted signal using t-$\\tau$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(\\tau)=e^{-2\\tau}u(\\tau),\\qquad h(t-\\tau)=e^{-5(t-\\tau)}u(t-\\tau)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use only the unit-step factors to set the bounds. The exponent values 2 and 5 change the integrand, but they do not create integration limits.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "u(\\tau)\\ne 0 \\text{ only when } \\tau\\ge 0,\\qquad u(t-\\tau)\\ne 0 \\text{ only when } t-\\tau\\ge 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The second condition is $\\tau$$\\le$ t. Combining it with $\\tau$$\\ge$ 0 gives a nonempty overlap exactly when t$\\ge$ 0.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t-\\tau\\ge 0 \\Longleftrightarrow \\tau\\le t,\\qquad 0\\le \\tau\\le t\\quad \\text{for } t\\ge 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the output has no causal overlap for $t<0$. For t$\\ge$ 0, the convolution integral should run from $\\tau$=0 to $\\tau$=t.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Unequal-Exponent Exponential Convolution",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Multiplying Unequal Exponential Terms Inside the Integral",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(t)=e$^{-2t}$u(t)$ and $h(t)=e$^{-5t}$u(t)$, simplify the product $x(\\tau)$$h(t-\\tau)$ on the causal overlap interval.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the same overlap interval from the previous step: for t$\\ge$ 0, the product is active only on 0$\\le$ $\\tau$$\\le$ t. On that interval, use the exponential parts of $x(\\tau)$ and $h(t-\\tau)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)=e^{-2\\tau}e^{-5(t-\\tau)},\\qquad 0\\le \\tau\\le t",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now distribute the shifted exponent carefully. The negative sign applies to both terms inside t-$\\tau$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "-5(t-\\tau)=-5t+5\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute that back into the product and combine the $\\tau$ exponents. Keep the t-only factor separate from the $\\tau$-dependent factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{-2\\tau}e^{-5(t-\\tau)}=e^{-2\\tau}e^{-5t+5\\tau}=e^{-5t}e^{(-2+5)\\tau}=e^{-5t}e^{3\\tau}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the simplified product is $e^{-5t}$$e^{3\\tau}$. Unlike the equal-exponent case, the integrand still contains a $\\tau$-dependent exponential factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Unequal-Exponent Exponential Convolution",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Evaluating the Unequal-Exponent Overlap Integral",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Evaluate int_0^t $e^{-5t}$$e^{3 \\tau}$ d $\\tau$ for $t \\ge 0$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the variable of integration. The integral is with respect to $\\tau$, so t is fixed during this step. That makes $e^{-5t}$ a constant factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_0^t e^{-5t}e^{3\\tau}\\,d\\tau=e^{-5t}\\int_0^t e^{3\\tau}\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now antidifferentiate the remaining $\\tau$-dependent exponential. Because the exponent is $3\\tau$, divide by $3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{-5t}\\int_0^t e^{3\\tau}\\,d\\tau=e^{-5t}\\left[\\frac{e^{3\\tau}}{3}\\right]_{\\tau=0}^{\\tau=t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Evaluate both limits. The upper limit replaces $\\tau$ with $t$, and the lower limit gives $e^0=1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{-5t}\\left(\\frac{e^{3t}}{3}-\\frac{e^0}{3}\\right)=\\frac{e^{-5t}(e^{3t}-1)}{3}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the evaluated overlap integral is $e^{-5t}(e^{3t}-1)/3$. Leave it in this factored form for now; rewriting it as a scaled difference of exponentials is the next algebra step.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Unequal-Exponent Exponential Convolution",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Simplifying to a Scaled Difference of Exponentials",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Simplify $e^{-5t}$($e^{3t}$-1)/3 as a scaled difference of exponentials.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the factored result from the evaluated integral. To get the scaled difference form, distribute $e^{-5t}$ across the parentheses while keeping the outside factor 1/3.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{e^{-5t}(e^{3t}-1)}{3}=\\frac{e^{-5t}e^{3t}-e^{-5t}}{3}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now combine the exponents in the first term. Multiplying exponentials with the same base means adding their exponents.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{-5t}e^{3t}=e^{(-5+3)t}=e^{-2t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute that simplification back into the expression. The lower-limit term stays negative, and the scale factor stays in the denominator.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{e^{-5t}e^{3t}-e^{-5t}}{3}=\\frac{e^{-2t}-e^{-5t}}{3}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the scaled difference of exponentials is ($e^{-2t}$-$e^{-5t}$)/3. Do not attach the causal unit step in this step; that belongs when writing the final output.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Unequal-Exponent Exponential Convolution",
        "section_index": 6,
        "section_title": "Writing the Final Result as a Causal Output",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find (x*h)(t) for $x(t)=e$^{-2t}$u(t)$ and $h(t)=e$^{-5t}$u(t)$, including the causal unit step.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is causal support. Since both signals are one-sided, there is no overlap before $t=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "(x*h)(t)=0,\\qquad t<0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For t$\\ge$ 0, use the overlap interval 0$\\le$ $\\tau$$\\le$ t and the scaled difference already obtained from the unequal-exponent integral.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "(x*h)(t)=\\frac{e^{-2t}-e^{-5t}}{3},\\qquad t\\ge 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "To write one causal formula instead of a piecewise result, attach $u(t)$. The unit step keeps the expression active for t$\\ge$ 0 and zero for $t<0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "(x*h)(t)=\\frac{e^{-2t}-e^{-5t}}{3}\\,u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This has the expected unequal-exponent form: a scaled difference of the two decays, with the causal unit step included in the final answer.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
